          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Starting the spiral"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make a "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "magic-ring"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "magic ring"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". Work 6 "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "double-crochet-uk"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "dc"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " into the ring. Pull the tail to close. Do not join. Place a "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "stitch-marker"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "stitch marker"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " in the first dc of this round. (6 dc.)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Round 2"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Work 2 dc in each of the 6 sts around. When you reach the stitch marker, move it to the first dc of this new round. (12 dc.)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Round 3"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "*2 dc in next st, 1 dc in next st*; repeat from * to * around. Move marker. (18 dc.)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Round 4"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "*2 dc in next st, 1 dc in each of next 2 sts*; repeat from * to * around. When you return to the marker, sl st to the next dc to close the final round. Fasten off. (24 dc.)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "craftChart",
        "attrs": {
          "slug": "crochet-motif-spiral-round"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finishing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weave in both tails. The tail at the magic ring should be woven through the central stitches in two different directions to prevent the ring from loosening over time. Block flat if needed."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Joining"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Join spiral circles the same way as joined-round circles: whip stitch or mattress stitch through the outer edges. The absence of a join seam on the face of the motif is the only visible difference."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Care"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Machine wash at 30 °C on a gentle cycle. Lay flat to dry."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What to try next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Spiral construction also works for amigurumi bodies, hats, and tubular bags. Once the spiral flat circle is comfortable, try working the same increase sequence in a colour-change yarn to see how the spiral construction handles the stripe jog differently from joined rounds."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
